💡 Explanation:

The views.py file contains the business logic of a Django application. A view receives an HTTP request, processes it, and returns an HTTP response.

✨ Example:

from django.http import HttpResponse

def home(request):
    return HttpResponse("Welcome to Django!")

💡 Explanation:

The render() function is used to combine an HTML template with data (context) and return it as an HTTP response to the user's browser.

✨ Example:

from django.shortcuts import render

def home(request):
    return render(request, "exam/homepage.html")

💡 Explanation:

The database configuration in Django is defined inside the DATABASES setting in the settings.py file. It contains information such as the database engine, database name, username, password, host, and port.

✨ Example:

# settings.py

DATABASES = {
    "default": {
        "ENGINE": "django.db.backends.sqlite3",
        "NAME": BASE_DIR / "db.sqlite3",
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

Java is a high-level, object-oriented programming language developed by Sun Microsystems (now Oracle). It is widely used for web applications, Android development, desktop software, and enterprise applications.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

Every Java application starts execution from the main() method. The Java Virtual Machine (JVM) looks for this method when running a program.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Program Started");
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The class keyword is used to define a class in Java. A class acts as a blueprint for creating objects.

✨ Example:

class Student {
    String name;
}

💡 Explanation:

System.out.println() is used to print output to the console in Java. After printing, it automatically moves the cursor to the next line.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

int is a primitive data type in Java used to store whole numbers (integers). It can store values from -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int age = 21;
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

In Java, every statement must end with a semicolon (;). If you forget it, the compiler will generate a syntax error.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 10;
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The new keyword is used to create an object (instance) of a class in Java. It allocates memory for the object and calls the class constructor.

✨ Example:

class Student {
    String name = "Ali";
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();
        System.out.println(s.name);
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

Single-line comments in Java start with //. Everything after // on the same line is ignored by the compiler.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // This is a single-line comment
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The void keyword indicates that a method does not return any value.

✨ Example:

public class Main {

    static void greet() {
        System.out.println("Welcome!");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        greet();
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The assignment operator (=) is used to assign a value to a variable. Do not confuse it with ==, which is used to compare two values for equality.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int marks = 95;
        System.out.println(marks);
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The for loop is used when you know how many times a block of code should execute. It consists of three parts: initialization, condition, and update.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            System.out.println(i);
        }
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The boolean data type is used to store logical values. It can have only two possible values: true or false.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean isPassed = true;
        System.out.println(isPassed);
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The static keyword makes a method or variable belong to the class rather than to individual objects. A static method can be called without creating an object of the class.

✨ Example:

public class Main {

    static void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        greet();
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The String class belongs to the java.lang package, which is automatically imported into every Java program. Because of this, you can use String without writing an import statement.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String message = "Hello, Java!";
        System.out.println(message);
    }
}

💡 Explanation:

The java.lang package is automatically imported into every Java program. It contains commonly used classes such as String, System, Math, Object, and Integer, so you can use them without writing an import statement.

✨ Example:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "Haider";
        System.out.println(name.length());
    }
}

